## 2.2 Factory Pattern

### Purpose

Define an interface for creating objects, but let subclasses or implementing classes decide which class to instantiate. Factory pattern promotes loose coupling by eliminating the need to bind application-specific classes into the code.

### Problem Statement

The system has two types of users: **Admin** and **Customer**. Each type:
- Extends the abstract [User](file:///c:/Users/Ahmed/IdeaProjects/Online-Book-Store/src/main/java/com/example/onlinebookstore/Models/User.java#6-27) class
- Requires different initialization parameters
- Has different responsibilities

Without a factory, every class that creates users would need conditional logic to determine which type to instantiate.

### Implementation

**Location**: [Models/UserFactory.java](file:///c:/Users/Ahmed/IdeaProjects/Online-Book-Store/src/main/java/com/example/onlinebookstore/Models/UserFactory.java)

**Complete Implementation**:

```java
package com.example.onlinebookstore.Models;

public class UserFactory {
    public static User createUser(String role, String username, 
                                   String password, String address, String phone) {
        if (role.equalsIgnoreCase("ADMIN")) {
            return new Admin(username, password);
        } else if (role.equalsIgnoreCase("CUSTOMER")) {
            return new Customer(username, password, address, phone);
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid role: " + role);
        }
    }
}
```

### Class Hierarchy

**User (Abstract Base Class)**:

```java
public abstract class User {
    protected int id;
    protected String username;
    protected String password;
    protected String role;

    public User(String username, String password, String role) {
        this.username = username;
        this.password = password;
        this.role = role;
    }

    // Getters and setters...
}
```

**Admin (Concrete User)**:

```java
public class Admin extends User {
    public Admin(String username, String password) {
        super(username, password, "ADMIN");
    }

    // Admin-specific methods can go here
}
```

**Customer (Concrete User)**:

```java
public class Customer extends User {
    private String address;
    private String phone;
    private Cart cart;

    public Customer(String username, String password, String address, String phone) {
        super(username, password, "CUSTOMER");
        this.address = address;
        this.phone = phone;
        this.cart = new Cart(); // Each customer gets a cart
    }

    // Getters and setters for address, phone, cart...
}
```

### Class Hierarchy Diagram

```
           User (Abstract)
            │
            │ extends
            │
    ┌───────┴───────┐
    │               │
  Admin         Customer
    │               │
    │               ├── address: String
    │               ├── phone: String
    │               └── cart: Cart
    │
```

### Usage in the System

**1. User Registration (in UserService)**:

```java
public class UserService {
    private UserDAO userDAO;

    public void registerCustomer(String username, String password, 
                                  String address, String phone) {
        // Using Factory Pattern
        Customer customer = new Customer(username, password, address, phone);
        userDAO.createUser(customer);
    }
}
```

**2. Loading Users from Database (in UserDAO)**:

```java
public class UserDAO {
    public User getUserByUsername(String username) {
        // Query database for user...
        // ResultSet rs contains: role, username, password, address, phone
        
        String role = rs.getString("role");
        String user = rs.getString("username");
        String pass = rs.getString("password");
        String addr = rs.getString("address");
        String ph = rs.getString("phone");
        
        // Use Factory to create appropriate user type
        User user = UserFactory.createUser(role, user, pass, addr, ph);
        user.setId(rs.getInt("id"));
        return user;
    }
}
```

**3. Type Checking in BookStoreFacade**:

```java
public class BookStoreFacade {
    private User currentLoggedInUser;
    
    public boolean isAdmin() {
        return currentLoggedInUser instanceof Admin;
    }
    
    public boolean isCustomer() {
        return currentLoggedInUser instanceof Customer;
    }
    
    // Admin-only methods
    public void addBook(Book book) {
        if (currentLoggedInUser instanceof Admin) {
            bookDAO.addBook(book);
        }
    }
    
    // Customer-only methods
    public void addBookToCart(Book book, int quantity) {
        if (currentLoggedInUser instanceof Customer) {
            ((Customer) currentLoggedInUser).getCart().addBook(book, quantity);
        }
    }
}
```

### Benefits

| Benefit | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **Encapsulation** | Object creation logic centralized in one place |
| **Loose Coupling** | Client code doesn't depend on concrete classes |
| **Extensibility** | Easy to add new user types (e.g., Moderator, Guest) |
| **Consistency** | All users created through the same interface |
| **Type Safety** | Validation in factory ensures only valid roles |

### Factory Pattern Flow

```
1. Application needs to create a user
        ↓
2. Calls UserFactory.createUser(role, ...)
        ↓
3. Factory checks role parameter
        ↓
4. If "ADMIN" → creates Admin object
   If "CUSTOMER" → creates Customer object
   If invalid → throws exception
        ↓
5. Returns User reference (polymorphism)
        ↓
6. Caller uses User interface without knowing concrete type
```

**[PLACEHOLDER: UserFactory Sequence Diagram]**

### Example Usage Scenarios

**Scenario 1: New Customer Registration**
```java
// In RegisterController
String username = usernameField.getText();
String password = passwordField.getText();
String address = addressField.getText();
String phone = phoneField.getText();

// UserService internally uses Customer constructor
userService.registerCustomer(username, password, address, phone);
```

**Scenario 2: User Login**
```java
// In LoginController
User user = facade.login(username, password); // Returns User (polymorphic)

if (user instanceof Admin) {
    loadAdminDashboard();
} else if (user instanceof Customer) {
    loadCustomerDashboard();
}
```

### Extending the Factory

If we wanted to add a new user type (e.g., Moderator):

```java
public class Moderator extends User {
    private String department;
    
    public Moderator(String username, String password, String department) {
        super(username, password, "MODERATOR");
        this.department = department;
    }
}

// Update Factory
public class UserFactory {
    public static User createUser(String role, String username, 
                                   String password, String address, String phone) {
        if (role.equalsIgnoreCase("ADMIN")) {
            return new Admin(username, password);
        } else if (role.equalsIgnoreCase("CUSTOMER")) {
            return new Customer(username, password, address, phone);
        } else if (role.equalsIgnoreCase("MODERATOR")) {
            return new Moderator(username, password, address); // address as department
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid role: " + role);
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 2.3 Creational Patterns Summary

The Online Book Store implements two creational patterns strategically:

**Singleton Pattern**:
- **DatabaseManager**: Ensures single database connection
- **SessionManager**: Maintains single user session

**Factory Pattern**:
- **UserFactory**: Creates appropriate user type (Admin/Customer) based on role

These patterns work together to provide:
1. **Resource Efficiency**: Single database connection
2. **Session Consistency**: Single user session across app
3. **Flexible User Creation**: Easy to add new user types
4. **Loose Coupling**: Controllers don't know concrete user types

**Pattern Integration**:

```
Controllers
    ↓
SessionManager (Singleton) → stores User
    ↓
UserFactory (Factory) → creates User (Admin or Customer)
    ↓
UserDAO → stores to Database
    ↓
DatabaseManager (Singleton) → provides Connection
```

